With Copilot coding agent, you can let Copilot handle tasks in the background — from updating documentation to improving test coverage — while you focus actively on the work that really needs your attention.

Before you can get started, an organization administrator will need to enable the agent in the repository you're working in - or if you have personal repositories owned by your own account, you can enable them yourself from your user settings: https://gh.io/copilot-coding-agent-repository-opt-in

Once a repository has been enabled, you can hand a task to Copilot by assigning an issue to Copilot, or asking Copilot to open a pull request from Copilot Chat. Copilot will:

- Immediately open a draft pull request and outline its plan
- Write code, and validate its changes with your automated tests and linter
- Request your review once it's done

As Copilot works, you can keep an eye on it with a new agent logs view that is integrated into the pull request.

To find out more about <%= Copilot::COPILOT_SWE_AGENT %>, head to the GitHub Docs: https://gh.io/copilot-coding-agent-docs

